WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.782
This is the narrated briefing for a
companion guide in the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:09.178
Workbook: Service Accounts,
from user accounts to group

4
00:00:09.178 --> 00:00:13.312
managed service accounts to
Microsoft Entra workload identities.

5
00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:15.874
The guide's idea fits in one line.

6
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:18.819
A service account's password
is the one nobody changes,

7
00:00:18.819 --> 00:00:20.912
because changing it breaks something.

8
00:00:20.912 --> 00:00:25.042
Every step in the guide replaces a
password someone has to keep with

9
00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:28.876
a credential that Windows, or
Microsoft Entra I D, keeps instead.

10
00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:33.527
Every fact and command in this
deck comes from Microsoft Learn,

11
00:00:33.527 --> 00:00:38.000
read on November ninth, 2026,
and the guide footnotes each one.

12
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.737
Rules that are the program's own, not
Microsoft's, are called design guidance.

13
00:00:42.737 --> 00:00:47.923
We'll go in four parts: why this matters,
what the kinds of service identity are,

14
00:00:47.923 --> 00:00:52.240
who decides and what they keep,
and how each migration runs.

15
00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:57.028
Here's the problem.

16
00:00:57.028 --> 00:01:00.246
The most common service
account in an older estate

17
00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:03.389
is an ordinary user account
that a service runs as.

18
00:01:03.389 --> 00:01:08.400
An administrator sets its password by
hand, under the domain's password policy,

19
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.252
and it's rotated only if
someone remembers to.

20
00:01:11.252 --> 00:01:14.607
When that account holds
a service principal name,

21
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.552
an S P N, it's highly
exposed to Kerberoasting.

22
00:01:17.552 --> 00:01:21.594
Microsoft Learn's overview of delegated
managed service accounts names

23
00:01:21.594 --> 00:01:25.503
Kerberoasting as the common issue
with traditional service accounts.

24
00:01:25.503 --> 00:01:31.140
Learn's Entra guidance on securing user-based
service accounts gives three ways to

25
00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.347
find them: an S P N on a user account,

26
00:01:33.347 --> 00:01:36.452
delegation, and a password
that never expires.

27
00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:41.396
Once you've found one, record every
host, service, application pool and

28
00:01:41.396 --> 00:01:46.166
scheduled task that uses it, every S
P N, and every group it belongs to.

29
00:01:46.166 --> 00:01:48.953
That record is what the
migration has to carry.

30
00:01:48.953 --> 00:01:50.700
So what could it become?

31
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.277
The guide compares twelve kinds of service

32
00:01:57.277 --> 00:02:00.025
identity in one table;
here's the short form.

33
00:02:00.025 --> 00:02:04.440
On premises, Microsoft Learn names
the user account used as a service,

34
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.320
the standalone managed service account,
the group managed service account,

35
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.812
and, new in Windows Server 2025, the
delegated managed service account.

36
00:02:13.812 --> 00:02:19.065
A standalone or group account has a
two-hundred-forty-byte random password that

37
00:02:19.065 --> 00:02:24.317
Windows changes every thirty days by default;
for a G M S A, the domain controllers

38
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:29.251
compute it from the K D S root key, and
the hosts allowed to retrieve it hold it.

39
00:02:29.251 --> 00:02:31.902
A D M S A ties its secret to the machine,

40
00:02:31.902 --> 00:02:35.350
and Learn says it helps
prevent Kerberoasting.

41
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.082
Virtual and computer accounts act
as the computer on the network,

42
00:02:39.082 --> 00:02:41.363
so you can't tell which service used them.

43
00:02:41.363 --> 00:02:46.488
In Microsoft Entra I D: an app registration
with a certificate you keep in a vault;

44
00:02:46.488 --> 00:02:51.459
system- and user-assigned managed
identities, whose credentials Azure keeps;

45
00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:56.274
an Azure Arc-enabled server's own
managed identity; a federated credential,

46
00:02:56.274 --> 00:02:59.458
signed by an outside issuer;
and agent identities,

47
00:02:59.458 --> 00:03:02.487
which take their credentials
from a blueprint.

48
00:03:02.487 --> 00:03:05.450
None of the cloud kinds speaks Kerberos.

49
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.025
Three things follow from that table.

50
00:03:11.025 --> 00:03:15.675
First, nothing in Microsoft Entra
I D speaks Kerberos for a service.

51
00:03:15.675 --> 00:03:20.981
A workload that authenticates with Kerberos
or N T L M to an on-premises resource,

52
00:03:20.981 --> 00:03:26.287
a file share, a SQL Server login mapped
to a Windows account, or a Kerberos-only

53
00:03:26.287 --> 00:03:31.513
application, keeps an Active Directory
identity until that resource changes.

54
00:03:31.513 --> 00:03:36.375
Second, a G M S A is a better
password, not a different protocol.

55
00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:40.905
It's long, random and rotated, but
it still exists, every host allowed

56
00:03:40.905 --> 00:03:45.600
to retrieve it holds it, and it works
only while Active Directory does.

57
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.607
Third, managed identities
are the end state

58
00:03:48.607 --> 00:03:51.363
only on Azure or on an Arc-enabled server.

59
00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:54.810
So a hybrid workload keeps
its G M S A for Kerberos

60
00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.000
and gets a cloud identity
for its cloud calls.

61
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.275
Learn also documents special cases.

62
00:04:00.275 --> 00:04:03.151
Failover clusters don't support G M S As,

63
00:04:03.151 --> 00:04:06.925
though services on the
Cluster service can use one.

64
00:04:06.925 --> 00:04:11.450
In Entra Domain Services, the
K D S root key is pre-created.

65
00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.868
Cloud Sync's agent runs as a G M S A,

66
00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:18.980
and Connect Sync's account can
be chosen only at installation.

67
00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.067
Now, who decides.

68
00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:27.119
The guide's decision
path is nine questions,

69
00:04:27.119 --> 00:04:30.255
asked in order, and the
first yes is the target.

70
00:04:30.255 --> 00:04:33.807
Each target lines up with a
disposition the application

71
00:04:33.807 --> 00:04:36.755
owner records in the
Non-Person Identity track.

72
00:04:36.755 --> 00:04:41.005
One: is the account disabled,
unused or without an owner?

73
00:04:41.005 --> 00:04:42.005
Retire it.

74
00:04:42.005 --> 00:04:46.667
Two: does the workload run on an Azure
resource, or is it moving to one?

75
00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:49.606
A managed identity,
user-assigned preferred.

76
00:04:49.606 --> 00:04:55.493
Three: does it run in C I C D or on a
platform with its own O I D C issuer?

77
00:04:55.493 --> 00:04:57.017
A federated credential.

78
00:04:57.017 --> 00:05:01.892
Four: does it stay on premises
but call Graph or Azure A P I's?

79
00:05:01.892 --> 00:05:06.717
The Arc-enabled server's identity, or
an app registration with a certificate.

80
00:05:06.717 --> 00:05:12.507
Five: does it need Kerberos, with a
Windows Server 2025 domain controller,

81
00:05:12.507 --> 00:05:16.656
every host on 2025, and no
unconstrained delegation?

82
00:05:16.656 --> 00:05:17.302
A D M S A.

83
00:05:17.302 --> 00:05:19.502
Six: otherwise, a G M S A.

84
00:05:19.502 --> 00:05:26.230
Seven and eight keep what must
stay, with an owner and an end date.

85
00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.105
Nine is the Kerberos island.

86
00:05:28.105 --> 00:05:32.569
This lines up with Microsoft Learn:
use a G M S A unless the service

87
00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:37.480
doesn't support one, then a standalone
account, then a user account.

88
00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.060
These are the program's
rules, design guidance,

89
00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.368
and they apply to a hand migration too.

90
00:05:46.368 --> 00:05:51.337
Every identity has two named technical
owners and a sponsor who is a federal

91
00:05:51.337 --> 00:05:54.413
employee mastered in H R;
a contractor can build

92
00:05:54.413 --> 00:05:57.331
or run a workload but
can't be its only sponsor.

93
00:05:57.331 --> 00:06:00.468
No account moves without
its reviewed disposition.

94
00:06:00.468 --> 00:06:04.587
The old account runs in parallel
for thirty days, then it's disabled,

95
00:06:04.587 --> 00:06:08.406
and it's deleted only later,
through the decommission process.

96
00:06:08.406 --> 00:06:12.102
No client secrets: a cloud
identity is a managed identity,

97
00:06:12.102 --> 00:06:15.641
a federated credential or a
certificate, in that order,

98
00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:19.417
and Microsoft Learn says not
to use password credentials.

99
00:06:19.417 --> 00:06:24.093
No privileged groups: Learn says service
accounts shouldn't be members of them,

100
00:06:24.093 --> 00:06:26.755
and each service should
have its own account.

101
00:06:26.755 --> 00:06:32.117
And changes are reviewed: in production,
each write is a plan approved by its hash.

102
00:06:32.117 --> 00:06:34.493
One place where Learn's own pages differ.

103
00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:39.155
Learn's Entra page on group managed service
accounts ends its move by deleting the

104
00:06:39.155 --> 00:06:42.035
old account, while its page
on setting up delegated

105
00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:44.504
accounts says never to
delete the original.

106
00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:47.220
The program follows the stricter reading.

107
00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:54.148
Every step carries its
federal requirement,

108
00:06:54.148 --> 00:06:58.270
the indicator it aligns to, and
the evidence the I S S O keeps.

109
00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:01.930
The inventory supports A
C two, account management,

110
00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.632
and C M eight, the component inventory.

111
00:07:04.632 --> 00:07:09.147
The K D S root key supports I A
five, authenticator management,

112
00:07:09.147 --> 00:07:13.571
with event forty-oh-four and the
key's start time as evidence.

113
00:07:13.571 --> 00:07:19.043
Creating the account supports A C six,
least privilege, I A five, and C M three,

114
00:07:19.043 --> 00:07:25.145
configuration change control: the evidence
is the approved plan and the read-back.

115
00:07:25.145 --> 00:07:30.084
Installing and switching supports least
privilege and privileged accounts:

116
00:07:30.084 --> 00:07:33.351
Test-A D Service Account on
each host, the S P N listing,

117
00:07:33.351 --> 00:07:35.858
and the service's managed-account check.

118
00:07:35.858 --> 00:07:38.746
The parallel run supports
disabling accounts.

119
00:07:38.746 --> 00:07:44.339
The cloud identities support I A nine,
service identification and authentication,

120
00:07:44.339 --> 00:07:47.257
which sits in no baseline
and is tailored in.

121
00:07:47.257 --> 00:07:50.754
The controls are from the NIST
special publication eight

122
00:07:50.754 --> 00:07:54.032
hundred fifty-three, revision
five, Moderate baseline.

123
00:07:54.032 --> 00:07:58.190
K S I's are fed-ramp twenty X
indicators for cloud service providers;

124
00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:03.770
which evidence supports which indicator is
the program's mapping, design guidance.

125
00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:14.096
Migration A moves a user account to a
G M S A, in Microsoft Learn's order:

126
00:08:14.096 --> 00:08:19.145
K D S root key, create, install,
switch, validate, retire.

127
00:08:19.145 --> 00:08:24.516
A1: read the account with Get-A D User,
its S P Ns, delegation and groups,

128
00:08:24.516 --> 00:08:30.083
and on each host read the service's
logon account with S C query config.

129
00:08:30.083 --> 00:08:33.858
A2: the forest needs one K D S root key.

130
00:08:33.858 --> 00:08:38.465
If there's none, a Domain Admin or
Enterprise Admin creates it once,

131
00:08:38.465 --> 00:08:42.561
and Learn's K D S page says
domain controllers wait up to ten

132
00:08:42.561 --> 00:08:46.657
hours before they allow a G M S
A, so the key replicates first.

133
00:08:46.657 --> 00:08:50.222
Learn offers a key backdated
ten hours for a test domain

134
00:08:50.222 --> 00:08:53.708
with one domain controller;
never use it in production.

135
00:08:53.708 --> 00:08:57.038
And Learn's containers
page says more than one

136
00:08:57.038 --> 00:09:00.457
key makes G M S As fail
after a password rotation.

137
00:09:00.457 --> 00:09:05.828
A3: create a group of the hosts, then
the G M S A with A E S two-fifty-six,

138
00:09:05.828 --> 00:09:09.882
which Learn's overview
says to always configure.

139
00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:12.323
Its name must be unique in the forest,

140
00:09:12.323 --> 00:09:15.333
and its password interval
can be set only now.

141
00:09:15.333 --> 00:09:19.795
A4: after a restart, install
it on each host and test it.

142
00:09:19.795 --> 00:09:24.880
Test-A D Service Account must return
true on every host before you switch.

143
00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:33.566
A5 happens in one change window,
because an S P N left on the old account

144
00:09:33.566 --> 00:09:38.243
while the service runs as the G M
S A breaks Kerberos to the service.

145
00:09:38.243 --> 00:09:41.229
Each S P N must have exactly one owner.

146
00:09:41.229 --> 00:09:47.380
Remove it from the old account, add it to
the G M S A, and list it with set S P N.

147
00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.368
Then change the workload's identity.

148
00:09:49.368 --> 00:09:52.226
A G M S A logs on with a blank password,

149
00:09:52.226 --> 00:09:56.168
which Windows fills in, as
Learn's Entra page says.

150
00:09:56.168 --> 00:09:59.344
For a Windows service, S
C config sets the account,

151
00:09:59.344 --> 00:10:02.442
and the managed-account
query must report true.

152
00:10:02.442 --> 00:10:06.558
A scheduled task gets a new principal
with the password logon type,

153
00:10:06.558 --> 00:10:10.000
as Learn's assessment pages do
it, and an I I S application

154
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.367
pool gets identity type three
with the blank password.

155
00:10:13.367 --> 00:10:16.320
The guide notes that
Learn has no single page

156
00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.193
showing that last one
end to end, so verify it.

157
00:10:19.193 --> 00:10:22.668
A6 is the thirty-day parallel
run: test end to end,

158
00:10:22.668 --> 00:10:26.679
keep the old account enabled,
and watch its logons stop.

159
00:10:26.679 --> 00:10:32.368
A7 disables the old account; deletion comes
later, through the decommission process.

160
00:10:32.368 --> 00:10:35.683
To roll back, switch the
workload back with the old

161
00:10:35.683 --> 00:10:39.100
password from the vault,
and move the S P Ns back.

162
00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.028
Migration B moves the
same kind of account to a

163
00:10:46.028 --> 00:10:50.125
delegated managed service
account, on Windows Server 2025.

164
00:10:50.125 --> 00:10:55.162
A D M S A supersedes the old account:
once the migration completes,

165
00:10:55.162 --> 00:11:00.674
the old password can't be used, its
S P Ns, delegation and authentication

166
00:11:00.674 --> 00:11:04.950
policy move to the D M S A, and
the old account is disabled.

167
00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.400
Check Microsoft Learn's limits first.

168
00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:11.009
At least one Windows Server
2025 domain controller,

169
00:11:11.009 --> 00:11:15.541
and every machine that uses the
account updated to support D M S As;

170
00:11:15.541 --> 00:11:19.150
one that isn't fails once
the old account is disabled.

171
00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:22.961
You can't migrate an M S A
or a G M S A to a D M S A,

172
00:11:22.961 --> 00:11:27.350
and unconstrained
delegation stops working.

173
00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:28.691
B1 creates the D M S A.

174
00:11:28.691 --> 00:11:34.305
Learn's set-up page then sets a state
attribute with a parameter the cmdlet doesn't

175
00:11:34.305 --> 00:11:39.500
have; for a migration, skip it, because
starting the migration sets the state.

176
00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:42.541
B2 starts the migration,
and on each host sets

177
00:11:42.541 --> 00:11:46.075
Learn's registry value and
restarts the one service.

178
00:11:46.075 --> 00:11:49.876
B3: Learn says to wait at
least two ticket lifetimes,

179
00:11:49.876 --> 00:11:52.500
fourteen days, and recommends four.

180
00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.125
The program's thirty days covers four.

181
00:11:55.125 --> 00:11:58.630
Then complete it, and never
delete the original account.

182
00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.909
Migration C starts when the
workload moves to Azure,

183
00:12:05.909 --> 00:12:11.080
or gains a call to Graph or an Azure A P I,
and only for what it reaches with OAuth.

184
00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:16.218
Microsoft Learn's security best practices
for application properties set the order:

185
00:12:16.218 --> 00:12:20.489
a managed identity whenever possible;
where the app doesn't run on Azure,

186
00:12:20.489 --> 00:12:24.397
a platform that manages its own
credentials, which is federation;

187
00:12:24.397 --> 00:12:28.232
otherwise a certificate from a
trusted certificate authority,

188
00:12:28.232 --> 00:12:31.055
kept in a vault; and never
a password credential.

189
00:12:31.055 --> 00:12:35.602
C1: a workload moving to Azure gets
a user-assigned managed identity,

190
00:12:35.602 --> 00:12:40.880
created with the Azure C L I and given
the narrowest role at the narrowest scope.

191
00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:44.043
It outlives its resources,
so it needs an owner.

192
00:12:44.043 --> 00:12:48.604
C2: on an Arc-enabled server,
use the server's own identity.

193
00:12:48.604 --> 00:12:52.891
Learn says only local
administrators and one local group

194
00:12:52.891 --> 00:12:56.642
can request its tokens, so
add the G M S A to that group.

195
00:12:56.642 --> 00:12:58.845
C3: on a host with no Arc agent,

196
00:12:58.845 --> 00:13:03.730
an app registration with a
certificate, with two named owners.

197
00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:08.006
C4: for C I C D or another
cloud, a federated credential,

198
00:13:08.006 --> 00:13:12.480
whose issuer, subject and
audience must match exactly.

199
00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:18.160
C5: after thirty days carrying traffic,
retire what the cloud path replaced.

200
00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:24.997
The guide lists the dangers
and the guard for each.

201
00:13:24.997 --> 00:13:30.389
Create a G M S A before the K D S root
key replicates, and password retrieval

202
00:13:30.389 --> 00:13:35.510
fails on hosts that ask another domain
controller: wait the ten hours.

203
00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:38.982
Create a second root key,
and G M S As fail after

204
00:13:38.982 --> 00:13:42.735
their next password
rotation: one key per forest.

205
00:13:42.735 --> 00:13:47.309
Leave an S P N on the old account,
and Kerberos to the service

206
00:13:47.309 --> 00:13:52.073
fails or falls back to N T L M: move
the S P Ns in the switch window.

207
00:13:52.073 --> 00:13:55.099
Switch a host that can't
retrieve the password,

208
00:13:55.099 --> 00:13:58.285
and the service won't start:
test every host first.

209
00:13:58.285 --> 00:14:00.560
Put a G M S A in a privileged group,

210
00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:05.285
and every host that retrieves its
password holds that privilege.

211
00:14:05.285 --> 00:14:10.850
Complete a D M S A migration too early, or
with a host not on Windows Server 2025,

212
00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:14.560
and the service fails on
the hosts it hasn't learned.

213
00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:18.410
Delete the old account during the
migration, and there's no way back.

214
00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:21.241
Put a client secret on
the new app registration,

215
00:14:21.241 --> 00:14:24.435
and you have a password in
a configuration file again.

216
00:14:24.435 --> 00:14:28.198
And a G M S A left behind
when Active Directory retires

217
00:14:28.198 --> 00:14:32.410
loses its credential with
the last domain controller.

218
00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:39.555
In production, the same
moves run as reviewed code,

219
00:14:39.555 --> 00:14:42.785
in the Non-Person Identity
developer workbook.

220
00:14:42.785 --> 00:14:45.785
Chapter 3 records each
account's disposition.

221
00:14:45.785 --> 00:14:49.701
Chapter 6 does Migration A as
a plan: create the G M S A,

222
00:14:49.701 --> 00:14:55.947
wait for the host change and the thirty
days, then disable the superseded account.

223
00:14:55.947 --> 00:14:59.584
It does Migration B as three
planned actions, create,

224
00:14:59.584 --> 00:15:04.660
start and complete, with every limit
Microsoft Learn sets checked first.

225
00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:08.836
Chapter 5 does Migration C, the
identity and its credential,

226
00:15:08.836 --> 00:15:13.523
and tests a real token exchange,
and Chapter 7 cuts its permissions.

227
00:15:13.523 --> 00:15:16.354
Chapter 6 also keeps
the island list and the

228
00:15:16.354 --> 00:15:19.185
reconciliation for what stays on Kerberos.

229
00:15:19.185 --> 00:15:21.760
What the code never does matters as much.

230
00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:27.122
It never runs a plan nobody approved,
because each plan is sent only by its hash.

231
00:15:27.122 --> 00:15:29.942
And it never creates a K D S root key,

232
00:15:29.942 --> 00:15:34.373
never runs undo or reset,
and never deletes an account.

233
00:15:34.373 --> 00:15:39.333
To try it, take Module 12 of the Non-Person
Identity learning path, which runs

234
00:15:39.333 --> 00:15:43.973
Learn's steps on stand-ins and the
kit's plans on fixtures, with no tenant.

235
00:15:43.973 --> 00:15:49.372
Then Chapter 6's second lab, in a test
domain, and the guide's checklist.

236
00:15:49.372 --> 00:15:50.200
Thank you.
